From d6b964f8341fd885ae337d72fb559014fee03e95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@localhost>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 19:38:31 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 26/26] Reject vertex input that Metal's vertex descriptor validation
 aborts on

Metal validates the vertex descriptor of a render pipeline (and the stage input descriptor of a
compute pipeline) when the pipeline is created, and an invalid descriptor is not an error but an
always-on assertion that aborts the process: "Attribute at index 1 references a buffer at index 29
that has no stride." (STEAMAC-3/X, the whole VM). Every buffer layout an attribute uses needs a
non-zero stride, whatever its step function, and a step rate that fits the step function
(constant: 0, per vertex: 1, per instance: not 0).

An attribute whose binding has no VkVertexInputBindingDescription (invalid usage, but guest apps
send it through Venus) got the default, stride-less layout; with no binding descriptions at all,
MVKGraphicsPipeline::addVertexInputToShaderConversionConfig() dereferenced a null binding
description, and the attribute loop read past the binding descriptions. Such pipelines now fail
with VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED, also with geometry shaders, whose object stage would read an
unbound Metal buffer.

Before creating the Metal pipeline, the vertex (or stage input) descriptor is checked like Metal
does, so any remaining inconsistency fails the pipeline instead of aborting. The stride given to a
zero-stride binding (the maximum offset + size of its attributes) is at least 1 and aligned like
other strides, and a translated binding for an attribute beyond its binding's stride that has no
Metal buffer index left fails the pipeline instead of using an invalid index.

Zero strides themselves were already handled: static zero strides become a constant step
function with the attributes' extent as stride, dynamic strides use MTLBufferLayoutStrideDynamic
(attributeStride 0 is accepted at bind time), and the geometry shader object stage fetches with
stride 0.
